    what an exhaustive walk gives, however many paths lead to a Varnode.
    f is clang 14 -O2 (-fno-stack-protector -fcf-protection=none
    -fno-jump-tables, linked -nostdlib -static -Ttext=0x403000 with stub
    callees e0 to e7 and ext): five switches of eight cases on s0 to s4,
    each case calling e0 to e7 and every other one stepping p, then
    p[j & 7] stored through:

        unsigned char buf[16]; buf[0] = a; unsigned char *p = buf;
        switch ((unsigned)(s0) % 8) { case 0: e0(s0); break;
            case 1: e1(s0); p++; break; ... default: e7(s0); p++; break; }
        ... the same for s1 to s4 ...
        p[i & 7] = j; if (buf[0]) return ext(buf[0]) + 1; return 5;

    The pointer resolves only after more than 4,096 values; a walk that
    gave up after 4,096 split buf into a char and a char[15] and indexed
    past the char.  Stage: P6 layout (stackstoreguard).  The walk remembers each
    Varnode at each depth, so it resolves, as on main.
-->
